The canonical-key technique generalized here was first implemented in
twemredis-py, which reproduced twemproxy's md5 key distribution inside the
client so applications could address sharded Redis instances directly, without
routing requests through a twemproxy process. Because a twemproxy shard was a
whole Redis instance, computing one canonical key per shard was enough to make
keys co-resident and intersectable.

Redis Cluster requires multi-key commands to share a hash slot rather than a
node, so intraslot pins one canonical key per slot instead of per shard, which
makes the guarantee hold under resharding as well.
